TLS support
===========

Build with a supported OpenSSL release:

  cmake -S . -B build-security -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DHAVE_SSL=ON
  cmake --build build-security --parallel 2
  ctest --test-dir build-security --output-on-failure

Use SSLSocketInitiator/SSLSocketAcceptor or
ThreadedSSLSocketInitiator/ThreadedSSLSocketAcceptor with the existing
Application, MessageStoreFactory and optional LogFactory interfaces.
Call start() and stop() as for the plaintext transports. setPassword()
provides the password for an encrypted certificate key.

Trust and identity
==================

TLS settings belong in [DEFAULT], except CertificateAcceptedPeerName,
which may also be specified separately in each [SESSION].

Initiators always verify the server certificate chain and the selected
SocketConnectHost, including a host selected during failover. DNS hosts
match certificate subject alternative names (SANs); numeric IPv4/IPv6
hosts must match an IP SAN. SNI is sent only for DNS names. A failed
handshake or verification prevents FIX traffic and responder attachment.
There is no CertificateVerifyLevel setting that disables server verification.

CertificationAuthoritiesFile is a PEM CA bundle.
CertificationAuthoritiesDirectory is an OpenSSL hashed CA directory.
When either is configured, only those roots are trusted; system roots are
not added. With neither configured, initiators use OpenSSL's default trust
paths, including its SSL_CERT_FILE/SSL_CERT_DIR environment overrides.
An explicitly empty or unreadable trust configuration fails startup.

Acceptors require ServerCertificateFile and, if the private key is separate,
ServerCertificateKeyFile. Initiators may optionally supply
ClientCertificateFile and ClientCertificateKeyFile. Certificates need the
appropriate TLS server/client extended key usage.

CertificateVerifyLevel controls client authentication on acceptors:

  0 (default): server authentication only; no client certificate requested.
  1: a valid client certificate is required.
  2: a client certificate is optional, but must verify if supplied.

Other values fail startup on all TLS transports. The setting's name is
CertificateVerifyLevel, not VerifyLevel. Levels 1 and 2 require explicitly
configured usable certification authorities on acceptors. Their trust is
isolated from system roots. Server-authentication-only TLS remains supported.

CertificateAcceptedPeerName optionally binds a FIX session to one exact DNS
or IP SAN in a verified client certificate. Wildcards and CommonName fallback
are not accepted. A missing certificate or mismatch rejects the initial
Logon before registration, responder attachment, sequence resets, refresh,
or Application::fromAdmin. Use this with client verification level 1 or 2.
An empty value disables this extra binding. It applies to TLS acceptors;
it does not authenticate peers on plaintext listeners.

TLS authenticates transport identities. The embedding Application must
still validate Logon credentials and authorize the requested account in
fromAdmin(), throwing RejectLogon on failure. Do not assume a shared CA
alone binds a certificate to a particular FIX SenderCompID.

Protocols and revocation
========================

SSLProtocol uses case-insensitive, whitespace-separated protocol names,
optionally prefixed by + or -. TLSv1_2 and TLSv1_3 are the usable versions
(TLSv1_3 requires OpenSSL 1.1.1+). The default and "all" obey a TLS 1.2
minimum. Invalid tokens and configurations allowing no TLS 1.2+ version
fail startup. Legacy SSL/TLS names cannot lower the minimum.

SSLCipherSuite controls OpenSSL's pre-TLS-1.3 cipher list; TLSCipherSuites
controls the TLS 1.3 list. If omitted, OpenSSL's cipher/security policy is
retained. QuickFIX adds no custom cipher policy.

CertificateRevocationListFile and CertificateRevocationListDirectory apply
to both initiators and acceptors. Files contain PEM CRLs; directories use
OpenSSL's hashed layout (`<hash>.r<number>`). Only CRLs are loaded from
revocation directories; certificates there never become trust anchors.
Empty CRL directories and configured load/flag failures abort startup.
CRL checks cover the entire verified chain; absent, expired or invalid
required CRLs fail the handshake. QuickFIX does not download CRLs.

Contexts own the trust/revocation store, including on legacy OpenSSL.
Name verification requires OpenSSL 1.0.2 or newer. Exact SAN-only session
binding requires native NEVER_CHECK_SUBJECT support (OpenSSL 1.1.0+)
and fails closed when unavailable. Legacy 1.0.2 DNS verification retains
OpenSSL's CommonName fallback when no DNS SAN is present. Deployments
should use a maintained OpenSSL release. Pre-1.1.0 protocol floors use
OpenSSL's protocol-disable options because its minimum-version API is absent.

Migration and disposable examples
=================================

Existing private-CA deployments must configure their CA explicitly and
issue certificates matching SocketConnectHost. Peers restricted to TLS
1.0/1.1 must upgrade. Adding the optional name string extends Session's
class size; rebuild consumers against the matching library and headers.
Existing public method signatures remain available.

The SSL unit build generates short-lived, test-only PKI in its ignored
test-runtime/certs directory using test/generate-test-pki.sh. To generate
the example files explicitly from the repository root:

  sh test/generate-test-pki.sh build-security/test-runtime/certs

The script refuses to overwrite existing keys. To renew expired fixtures,
remove only that generated certs directory and rebuild. Sample paths in
bin/cfg/executor.cfg and tradeclient.cfg assume execution from bin/.
These credentials are local test fixtures, never production identities.

Production certificate issuance, secure key storage, renewal, expiry
monitoring and timely CRL updates belong to the deployment. Contexts load
files at initialization; restart/recreate the transport to apply renewed
certificates, trust or CRLs, preserving FIX sequence/store files.
